  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What causes acid rain?",
      "a": "Sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from burning fuels in power stations, factories and vehicles. In the air they turn into sulfuric and nitric acids that dissolve in rain.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does acid rain come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does acid rain harm lakes and fish?",
      "a": "It lowers the pH of lakes and releases harmful aluminium from soils. Many fish, frogs and insects cannot survive or breed in water that is too acidic.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do fish die from acid rain?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does acid rain damage statues?",
      "a": "Limestone and marble are made of calcium carbonate, which reacts with acid and slowly dissolves, so carvings lose their detail.",
      "asks": [
        "How does acid rain affect buildings?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is normal rain acidic?",
      "a": "Slightly. Clean rain dissolves a little carbon dioxide from the air and has a pH of about 5.6. Rain more acidic than that is called acid rain.",
      "asks": [
        "What pH is rain?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Has acid rain been reduced?",
      "a": "Yes. Scrubbers that remove sulfur from power station smoke, low-sulfur fuels and catalytic converters have cut acid rain greatly in Europe and North America since the 1980s.",
      "asks": [
        "Is acid rain still a problem?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Clean rain is naturally slightly acidic, about pH 5.6, because carbon dioxide dissolves in it.",
    "Sulfur dioxide pollution in Europe and North America has fallen by well over three quarters since 1980."
  ],
